<BaseLayout title="Indian e-waste rules in plain language" description="A careful public information summary of India's E-Waste Management Rules and official sources.">
  <section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Rules in plain language</p><h1>Understand the framework. Check the official text.</h1><p class="lede">India’s E-Waste (Management) Rules, 2022 establish a formal framework that includes registration and extended producer responsibility for regulated entities.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section-tight"><div class="container content-grid">
    <article class="prose">
      <div class="note"><strong>Important:</strong><p>This is an educational summary, not legal advice. The rules and amendments may change. For compliance decisions, use the current Gazette notification and relevant regulator guidance.</p></div>
      <h2>What the rules broadly do</h2>
      <p>The rules provide a framework for environmentally sound management of specified electrical and electronic equipment and e-waste. The official materials describe registration and extended producer responsibility requirements for manufacturers, producers, refurbishers, and recyclers.</p>
      <h2>What this means for households</h2>
      <p>This site does not translate business registration or producer obligations into new household legal duties. For an unwanted device, check a product maker’s take-back route or current official facility information, and confirm details before handover.</p>
      <h2>Do not confuse waste streams</h2>
      <p>Waste batteries are addressed through a separate Battery Waste Management Rules framework. Some products or materials may also be covered by other rules. Follow the guidance that applies to the specific item.</p>
      <h2>Where to verify the current position</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.moef.gov.in/rules-regulations-3" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MoEFCC rules and regulations page</a> — official E-Waste Rules and amendments.</li>
        <li><a href="https://cpcb.nic.in/uploads/Projects/E-Waste/e-waste_rules_2022.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CPCB-hosted E-Waste (Management) Rules, 2022 (PDF)</a>.</li>
        <li><a href="https://cpcb.nic.in/uploads/Projects/E-Waste/FAQ_ewaste_23012024.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CPCB FAQ on the 2022 Rules (PDF)</a>.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.mpcb.gov.in/en/node/4297" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MPCB e-waste page</a> — Maharashtra-specific rules, procedures, and facility sources.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="small-note">This source list was checked on 16 November 2026. The MoEFCC page should be rechecked before publishing a legal-summary update.</p>
    </article>
    <aside class="side-card"><h2 class="footer-heading">Remember</h2><p>Formal obligations differ by role. This site is for general public awareness, not compliance determinations.</p><a href="/guide/">Read the safe handling guide →</a></aside>
  </div></section>
